  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSoutheasterly flow will take hold across the area tonight, driving \nup dew points, and keeping overnight lows milder than previous \nevenings. Moisture from the remnants of Isaias are progged to move \ninto the region today, with precipitation reaching the mountains and \nparts of SE Ohio/Mid Ohio Valley first. Parts of the WV lowlands, in \nfavored downslope regions, should remain drier to start, before flow \nveers to a more southerly/southwesterly direction, and the \natmospheric column gradually saturates with time. As the pressure \ngradient tightens across the area, winds will be on the increase \ntoday, with gusts in the lowlands generally 20-25 mph. Across the \nmountains, where stronger winds aloft will interact with the higher \nterrain, wind gusts will have the potential to reach or exceed \nadvisory criteria at times, particularly in favored downslope \nregions, and a wind advisory has been issued for these areas. \n\nThe low will move east across the area Sunday night into Monday, \nwith showery conditions lingering, particularly across the \nmountainous counties as a moist northwesterly flow takes hold. High \npressure will then build in for early next week behind the departing \nlow, with a brief break in the rainy weather, before another frontal \nboundary affects the area mid week, providing for another chance of \nlight precipitation, followed by another system late in the week. \n\nMountainous locations and SE Ohio could see a good soaking rain from \nthis. For now, mainly SE Ohio and the Mid Ohio Valley region is in a \nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall. But the period will need to be \nwatched for the potential for flooding of area creeks and streams, \nparticularly in the eastern counties and southeast Ohio. Dry \nconditions leading up to the event should hopefully prevent much in \nthe way of significant or overly widespread flooding issues.   \n\nIn addition, the risk for tree fall and power outages will be \nincreased across the area during the period, particularly as the \nground becomes saturated and winds increase.",
